    historyContent: {
      origins: 'Twenty-five is the Irish national card game and underlies the Canadian game Forty-fives. It descends from the old Scottish game Maw, documented from 1548 and fashionable at the English court under James I. Charles Cotton in 1674 described the ancestor as Five Cards, with the trump 5 (five fingers) as highest. The game reappeared in the early 19th century alongside Spoil Five and Forty-five; it was mentioned in the Dublin Penny Journal in 1833 and by 1858 Irish sources called Twenty-Five and Forty-Five our own old games. The first detailed rules were published in 1876 by H.E. Heather. Twenty-five eventually became the national card game of Ireland.',
      originCountries: [
        'Ireland',
      ],
      timeline: [
        '19th century: descended from Maw and Spoil Five; modern Irish form widely played in pubs and at home.',
      ],
      evolution: 'Spawned Auction Forty-Fives in Canada.',
      cultural: 'Central to Irish pub culture.',
    },
